# Production build config — read automatically by `vite build`
# (mode=production), and by scripts/deploy-browser.sh. Public URLs
# only, no secrets. A shell-exported var of the same name wins over
# anything here.
#
# Sibling file .env.staging holds the staging deployment
# (staging-gemma.msl.ubc.ca, published next to this one under
# /space/web/gemma-ui/). Neither file is loaded for the other's build,
# so a value that should hold for both has to appear in both.
#
# Target: https://gemma.msl.ubc.ca/
#         served from /space/web/gemma-ui/production by Apache 2.4
#         (plain static files — no Tomcat, no container).
#
# This file named gemma2.msl.ubc.ca until 2026-09-03. DEPLOY_DEST did
# not change with it: the build still publishes to the same docroot, so
# serving it as gemma.msl.ubc.ca is a vhost/DNS matter on the server,
# not a different deploy. Confirm the vhost for gemma.msl.ubc.ca points
# at this docroot before trusting a deploy to have gone live.
#
# The facts recorded below were measured against gemma2.msl.ubc.ca (a
# DNS alias for moe.msl.ubc.ca — the docroot is an NFS mount, but the
# Apache serving it is moe's). They are the reasoning, not fresh
# measurements of the new host: re-probe gemma.msl.ubc.ca before
# relying on any of them, starting with which Apache answers for it.

# Mount point. The app is served from the origin root, so this is "/"
# and Vite emits root-absolute asset URLs (/assets/…).
#
# It was /gemmaui/ until 2026-08-25, when the docroot was remapped to
# the root. The build is not portable across that change: a bundle
# built for /gemmaui/ asks for /gemmaui/assets/… and 404s at the root
# (and vice versa). Changing this line means rebuilding and
# redeploying, not just moving files.
VITE_BASE_PATH=/

# Where deploy-browser.sh publishes. Unchanged by the remap — the same
# directory is simply mounted at / now instead of /gemmaui/. Must
# contain nothing but this app's build output: the deploy runs
# rsync --delete.
DEPLOY_DEST=/space/web/gemma-ui/production

# Absolute origin for URLs a human follows or pastes — the gemmapy /
# curl snippets on the Browser page, and rewriting ontology term URIs
# that point at the old gemma.msl.ubc.ca host. Same origin that serves
# this app, but spelled absolutely on purpose: a relative path is
# meaningless once pasted into a terminal. https, not http — gemma2
# 302s http → https, and a redirected snippet is a confusing thing to
# hand someone; assumed to hold for gemma.msl.ubc.ca too, worth a
# `curl -I` to confirm.
VITE_GEMMA_BASE_URL=https://gemma.msl.ubc.ca

# Curation-app origin for the AppBar "Curation" tab / footer link.
# Deliberately unset — falls back to http://localhost:5175, so those
# links only work on a machine running the curation dev server. Set it
# once the curation app has a deployed URL.
# VITE_CURATION_URL=

# NOT set here, each deliberately:
#
# VITE_GEMMA_API_URL — unset means the /rest/v2 default in
#   src/api/base.ts, resolved against the app's own origin. Because the
#   app is served from the Gemma host itself, that IS that host's real
#   REST API (verified on gemma2: GET /rest/v2/info → 200), so the
#   default is exactly right and naming it would only add a second
#   place to keep in sync. It holds for gemma.msl.ubc.ca only if that
#   host serves Gemma 2.0's /rest/v2 — check it does.
#
#   This is also why the app is hosted here rather than on another box:
#   Gemma's Tomcat CORS filter allow-lists exactly one origin — itself
#   — and 403s the preflight from anywhere else (probed 2026-08-17 on
#   gemma2).
#   From gemma2 there is nothing to work around. From anywhere else
#   this deployment would need an Origin-stripping reverse proxy first.
#
# VITE_GEMMA_PUBLIC_URL — the internet-reachable Gemma base, used for
#   URLs handed to third parties (the UCSC track link). Unset because
#   the app's own origin is already public https, which is what
#   publicGemmaUrl() falls back to. Only needed on a public plain-http
#   host, where that heuristic can't tell public from internal.
#
# GEMMA_BASE_URL — configures the dev server's /rest proxy and nothing
#   else. A production build has no proxy; the relative paths above go
#   straight to the serving origin.
